    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2 tests, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe locality derivation now prefers the postcode, falling back to the city table.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`PostcodeRanges.java`** \u2014 added `regionFor(String postcode)`: the reverse lookup that returns the region whose range contains the postcode (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099), or `null` when the postcode is absent, not four digits, or in no known range.\n\n2. **`Locality.java`** \u2014 added `of(String city, String postcode)`: consults `PostcodeRanges.regionFor(postcode)` first and only falls back to the city-to-region table when that yields no region. Kept the existing single-arg `of(city)` intact, since `ValidatePostcode` still validates a postcode against its *city's* region.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the `locality` mapping expression now passes both `owner.getCity()` and `owner.getPostcode()`.\n\n**Why it satisfies the spec:** known cities carrying an in-range postcode still derive the same region (e.g. Sydney + 2000 \u2192 NSW), while a city that shares a name but carries a differently-ranged postcode is now disambiguated by the postcode (e.g. an unknown city + 3000 \u2192 VIC). Both new acceptance tests (`coreRegionFromPostcodeRange`, `functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity`) are green.",
